<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mrs. Oakley is a 64-year-old woman with pernicious anemia on monthly vitamin B12 injections, autoimmune atrophic gastritis, iron deficiency on alternate-day oral iron, and chronic low back pain, who asked for a same-day visit. For two days she has had burning on urination, frequency every hour, urgency, and lower abdominal discomfort. She has had no fever, chills, flank pain, nausea, vaginal discharge, or blood in the urine. She has not had a bladder infection for many years and has not taken antibiotics in the past three months. She has taken no naproxen this week. She had a normal colonoscopy three weeks ago. Her feet still feel slightly numb at the toes but the tingling is gone. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Bladder and urethra</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Two days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Burning on urination with urgency</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Moderate; waking twice at night to void</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Constant</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>No recent antibiotics; no catheter or instrumentation of the urinary tract</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>More fluids help a little</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Lower abdominal discomfort. Denies fever, flank pain, vomiting, vaginal symptoms, and hematuria</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

