<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute uncomplicated cystitis (N30.00)</content>. Typical symptoms without fever, flank pain, or costovertebral tenderness, so pyelonephritis is improbable. eGFR above 30 allows nitrofurantoin. [S]<list><item>Nitrofurantoin monohydrate/macrocrystals 100 mg twice daily with food for five days.</item><item>No fluoroquinolone: they are reserved for other uses, can worsen peripheral neuropathy, and bind to iron tablets.</item><item>Urine culture only if symptoms persist or recur.</item><item>Call for fever, flank pain, vomiting, or no improvement in 48 hours.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pernicious anemia on maintenance B12 (D51.0)</content>. Neuropathy improving; nitrofurantoin for five days carries little risk of neuropathy. [S]<list><item>Continue monthly injections; next one as scheduled.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Iron deficiency on alternate-day oral iron (E61.1)</content>. No interaction with nitrofurantoin. [S]<list><item>Continue iron every other day.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: International Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Treatment of Acute Uncomplicated Cystitis and Pyelonephritis in Women: A 2010 Update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America and the European Society for Microbiology and Infectious Diseases (2011)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone check in three days. Five-month review in July as planned. Next scheduled visit 1925-07-06: five-month pernicious anemia, iron, neuropathy, and back pain review. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
